s from A. The triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC, scaled down by a factor of 70/100 = 0.7. Therefore, the base DE would be 0.7 times the base BC. But BC is 60, so DE would be 0.7*60 = 42. That matches the previous result.\n\nTherefore, the distance between D and E is 42 units. That seems right.\n\nWait, but why is triangle ADE similar to ABC? Because if we have points D and E such that AD/AB = AE/AC = 0.7, then by the basic proportionality theorem, DE is parallel to BC and DE = 0.7*BC. But in this case, DE is not the base but connects two points on the legs. Wait, actually, if DE connects points on AB and AC, then if AD/AB = AE/AC, then DE is parallel to BC? Wait, in an isosceles triangle, if you take points along the legs at the same proportional distance from the apex, then the segment connecting them is parallel to the base. So, in that case, DE would be parallel to BC and scaled by the same factor. Since AD = 70, AB = 100, the scaling factor is 0.7, so DE = 0.7*BC = 0.7*60 = 42. Hence, the distance is 42. That's another way to see it, which confirms the coordinate method.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 42.\n\nBut let me check with another method using the inradius and coordinates.\n\nAlternatively, since the inradius is 30\u221a91 /13, the center of the incircle (incenter) is located at a distance of r from each side. In an isosceles triangle, the incenter lies along the altitude from the apex to the base. So, the coordinates of the incenter would be (0, r'), where r' is the distance from the base to the incenter. Wait, but inradius is the distance from the incenter to any side. Wait, in an isosceles triangle, the inradius can be found as we did before.\n\nBut maybe using coordinates, the incenter is at (0, r), where r is the inradius. Wait, no. Wait, in the coordinate system we set up earlier, the inradius is the distance from the incenter to the base BC, which is along the y-axis. But actually, the inradius is the distance from the incenter to any side, including the lateral sides.\n\nWait, in our coordinate system, the incenter is located at (0, r_y), where r_y is the y-coordinate. But let's compute the inradius. Wait, we already did: r = 30\u221a91 /13. So, the inradius is the distance from the incenter to each side, which in the coordinate system would mean the incenter is at (0, r), but wait, no. Wait, the inradius is the distance to the sides, but in the coordinate system, the altitude is h = 10\u221a91. The inradius is r = A/s = 300\u221a91 /130 = 30\u221a91 /13 \u2248 30*9.539 /13 \u2248 286.17 /13 \u2248 22.01. But the altitude is h = 10\u221a91 \u2248 10*9.539 \u2248 95.39. So, the inradius is about 22.01, so the incenter is located at (0, h - r) \u2248 (0, 95.39 - 22.01) \u2248 (0, 73.38). Wait, but in our previous coordinate system, points D and E are at ( -21, 3\u221a91 ) and (21, 3\u221a91 ). Let's compute 3\u221a91: \u221a91\u22489.539, so 3\u221a91\u224828.617. So, their y-coordinate is approximately 28.617, which is lower than the inradius center at approximately 73.38. Wait, that seems inconsistent. Wait, perhaps my coordinate system is different.\n\nWait, in our coordinate system, the base BC is on the x-axis from (-30, 0) to (30, 0), and the apex A is at (0, h) where h = 10\u221a91 \u224895.39. The inradius is the distance from the incenter to each side, so the incenter must be located at (0, r), where r is the inradius, but only if the triangle is equilateral. Wait, no. In an isosceles triangle, the incenter lies along the altitude, so the x-coordinate is 0, and the y-coordinate can be found as h - r. Wait, actually, the inradius is the distance from the incenter to the base BC, which is along the x-axis. The distance from the incenter to the base BC is the y-coordinate of the incenter. Similarly, the distance from the incenter to the lateral sides AB and AC can be found, but in a triangle, the inradius is the same for all sides.\n\nWait, perhaps the incenter is located at (0, r). Wait, but if the base BC is on the x-axis, then the distance from the incenter (0, y) to BC is y, which must equal the inradius r. But also, the distance from the incenter to AB must also be r. Let's compute the coordinates of the incenter.\n\nIn general, the incenter coordinates can be found as a weighted average of the vertices. For a triangle with vertices at (x\u2081,y\u2081), (x\u2082,y\u2082), (x\u2083,y\u2083), and sides opposite these vertices of lengths a, b, c, the incenter is at ( (a x\u2081 + b x\u2082 + c x\u2083)/(a + b + c), (a y\u2081 + b y\u2082 + c y\u2083)/(a + b + c) ). \n\nIn our case, the triangle has vertices A(0, h), B(-30, 0), C(30, 0). The lengths opposite to these vertices are BC = 60 opposite A, AC = 100 opposite B, and AB = 100 opposite C. Wait, actually, the formula uses the lengths of the sides opposite the respective vertices. So, vertex A is opposite side BC (length 60), vertex B is opposite side AC (length 100), vertex C is opposite side AB (length 100). Therefore, the incenter coordinates are:\n\nx = (a x_A + b x_B + c x_C)/(a + b + c) = (60*0 + 100*(-30) + 100*30)/(60 + 100 + 100) = (0 - 3000 + 3000)/260 = 0/260 = 0.\n\ny = (a y_A + b y_B + c y_C)/(a + b + c) = (60*h + 100*0 + 100*0)/260 = 60h /260 = (60*10\u221a91)/260 = (600\u221a91)/260 = (60\u221a91)/26 = (30\u221a91)/13, which is equal to the inradius r. Wait, but that's the y-coordinate of the incenter. So, the incenter is at (0, r), where r = 30\u221a91/13. Which is approximately 22.01, as before. But in our coordinate system, the incenter is at (0, r), so (0, ~22.01). However, points D and E are at y \u224828.617, which is higher up. Wait, that seems contradictory. If the inradius is the distance from the incenter to the sides, then the points of tangency should be at a distance r from the incenter. But according to our earlier calculation, points D and E are at y \u224828.617, which is higher than the incenter's y-coordinate of ~22.01. That can't be, because the points of tangency should lie on the sides of the triangle, and the inradius is the distance from the incenter to the sides. Wait, maybe there's an error in my coordinate system reasoning.\n\nWait, let's double-check. If the incenter is at (0, r), then the distance from the incenter to the base BC (which is along the x-axis) is indeed r. The distance from the incenter to the lateral sides AB and AC should also be r. Let's verify this.\n\nTake side AB. The equation of side AB can be found using the two points A(0, h) and B(-30, 0). The slope of AB is (0 - h)/(-30 - 0) = -h / (-30) = h/30. So, the equation is y - 0 = (h/30)(x + 30). So, y = (h/30)x + h. The distance from the incenter (0, r) to this line should be equal to r.\n\nThe formula for the distance from a point (x\u2080, y\u2080) to the line ax + by + c = 0 is |ax\u2080 + by\u2080 + c| / sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2). Let's write the equation of AB in standard form. Starting from y = (h/30)x + h, subtract y: (h/30)x - y + h = 0. So, coefficients are a = h/30, b = -1, c = h.\n\nDistance from (0, r) to AB is |(h/30)(0) + (-1)(r) + h| / sqrt( (h/30)^2 + (-1)^2 ) = | -r + h | / sqrt( h\u00b2/900 + 1 ) = |h - r| / sqrt( (h\u00b2 + 900)/900 ) = |h - r| / ( sqrt(h\u00b2 + 900)/30 ) ) = 30|h - r| / sqrt(h\u00b2 + 900).\n\nBut this distance should equal the inradius r. Therefore:\n\n30|h - r| / sqrt(h\u00b2 + 900) = r\n\nWe know h = 10\u221a91, and r = 30\u221a91 /13.\n\nLet's compute h - r = 10\u221a91 - (30\u221a91)/13 = (130\u221a91 - 30\u221a91)/13 = (100\u221a91)/13.\n\nThen, |h - r| = 100\u221a91 /13.\n\nsqrt(h\u00b2 + 900) = sqrt( (10\u221a91)^2 + 900 ) = sqrt(100*91 + 900) = sqrt(9100 + 900) = sqrt(10000) = 100.\n\nTherefore, the left side becomes 30*(100\u221a91 /13)/100 = (30\u221a91)/13 = r, which matches. Therefore, the distance from the incenter to AB is indeed r. That checks out.\n\nSo, the incenter is at (0, r) where r = 30\u221a91/13. The points of tangency D and E on AB and AC are located at (-21, 3\u221a91) and (21, 3\u221a91). Let me check the distance from the incenter to point D.\n\nThe distance between (0, r) and (-21, 3\u221a91) should be equal to the inradius if D is the point of tangency. Wait, but the inradius is the distance from the incenter to the side AB, which is a line, not necessarily the distance from the incenter to the point D. However, the point D is the point where the incircle touches AB, so the line segment from the incenter to D is perpendicular to AB and has length r.\n\nLet's compute the vector from the incenter (0, r) to D (-21, 3\u221a91). The vector is (-21 - 0, 3\u221a91 - r) = (-21, 3\u221a91 - r). Let's compute the length of this vector.\n\nFirst, compute r = 30\u221a91 /13 \u2248 30*9.539/13 \u2248 22.01. 3\u221a91 \u22483*9.539\u224828.617. So, 3\u221a91 - r \u224828.617 -22.01\u22486.607. Then, the vector is (-21, ~6.607). The length is sqrt( (-21)^2 + (6.607)^2 ) \u2248 sqrt(441 + 43.65) \u2248 sqrt(484.65) \u224822.01, which is approximately equal to r. So, that checks out. Therefore, the distance from the incenter to D is indeed r, which makes sense since D is the point of tangency.\n\nTherefore, points D and E are correctly calculated at (-21, 3\u221a91) and (21, 3\u221a91). The distance between them is sqrt( (21 - (-21))^2 + (3\u221a91 - 3\u221a91)^2 ) = sqrt(42^2 + 0) = 42. So, that's confirmed.\n\nAlternatively, using the similarity of triangles ADE and ABC gives the same result. Since AD/AB = 70/100 = 0.7, the ratio is 0.7, so DE = 0.7*BC = 0.7*60 = 42. Therefore, this method is much quicker.\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points of tangency on the lateral sides is 42.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe distance between the points of tangency located on the lateral sides is \\boxed{42}.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the distance between the points of tangency located on the lateral sides of an isosceles triangle with lateral sides of 100 units and a base of 60 units, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the semi-perimeter (s) of the triangle:**\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{100 + 100 + 60}{2} = 130\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the area (A) of the triangle:**\n   - The height (h) of the triangle can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:\n     \\[\n     h = \\sqrt{100^2 - 30^2} = \\sqrt{10000 - 900} = \\sqrt{9100} = 10\\sqrt{91}\n     \\]\n   - The area is then:\n     \\[\n     A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 60 \\times 10\\sqrt{91} = 300\\sqrt{91}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the inradius (r):**\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{A}{s} = \\frac{300\\sqrt{91}}{130} = \\frac{30\\sqrt{91}}{13}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the points of tangency on the lateral sides:**\n   - The distance from the apex to each point of tangency on the lateral sides is given by \\( s - \\text{base} \\):\n     \\[\n     s - 60 = 130 - 60 = 70\n     \\]\n   - These points are 70 units from the apex along each lateral side.\n\n5. **Use similarity of triangles:**\n   - The points of tangency divide the lateral sides in the ratio 70:30. The segment connecting these points is parallel to the base and scaled by the same ratio.\n   - The distance between the points of tangency is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Distance} = \\left( \\frac{70}{100} \\right) \\times 60 = 0.7 \\times 60 = 42\n     \\]\n\nThus, the distance between the points of tangency located on the lateral sides is \\(\\boxed{42}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify Key Points:**\n    - Let $M$ and $N$ be the points of tangency of the incircle with the lateral sides $AB$ and $AC$ of the isosceles triangle $ABC$.\n    - Let $K$ be the point of tangency with the base $BC$.\n\n2. **Calculate Segment Lengths:**\n    - Given that the incircle is tangent to $AB$ and $AC$ at $M$ and $N$, the segments of $AB$ and $AC$ defined by these tangent points are equal. Thus, $AM = AN$.\n    - The total length of side $AB = AC = 100$ and the length of the base $BC = 60$.\n    - Using the tangent-segment property, we know that the segments from vertex $A$ to the points of tangency $M$ and $N$ are equal to the remaining parts of $AB$ and $AC$ reduced by the segments from the endpoints of the base $BC$ to $K$.\n    \n      Let the length of $BK = CK = x$ and $AK = y$. \n      \n      Triangle vertices and tangency points partition the sides as follows:\n      \\[\n      AB = AM + MB,\n      \\]\n      \\[\n      AM = AB - MB,\n      \\]\n      since $MB = BK = 30$.\n      \n      Therefore, considering that $AM = 100 - 30 = 70$, we have:\n      \\[\n      AM = 70.\n      \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the Distance Between Points $M$ and $N$:**\n    - By the similarity of triangles:\n      - Triangle $ABC$ and smaller triangle $AMN$ are similar because $AMN$ is created by tangents drawn from $A$ to the circle.\n      \n      The similarity ratio between corresponding sides of $\\triangle AMN$ and $\\triangle ABC$ is given by the lengths:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{MN}{BC} = \\frac{AM}{AB}.\n      \\]\n\n    - Substitute the known lengths:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{MN}{60} = \\frac{70}{100}.\n      \\]\n      \n      Therefore, solving for $MN$:\n      \\[\n      MN = 60 \\cdot \\frac{70}{100} = 42.\n      \\]\n\n4. **Final Answer:**\n    - The distance between points $M$ and $N$ is:\n    \n    \\[\n    \\boxed{42}\n    \\]\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find \\(\\lim _{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{4 x^{3}+x^{2}-2}{3 x^{2}+5 x-2}\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the limit as x approaches infinity of the function (4x\u00b3 + x\u00b2 - 2)/(3x\u00b2 + 5x - 2). Hmm, limits at infinity, right? Let me remember how to approach these kinds of problems. I think when dealing with rational functions (polynomial divided by polynomial), the behavior at infinity depends on the degrees of the numerator and denominator. \n\nFirst, let me check the degrees of both the numerator and the denominator. The numerator here is 4x\u00b3 + x\u00b2 - 2. The highest power of x here is x\u00b3, so the degree of the numerator is 3. The denominator is 3x\u00b2 + 5x - 2. The highest power there is x\u00b2, so the degree of the denominator is 2. \n\nI remember that if the degree of the numerator is higher than the degree of the denominator, the limit as x approaches infinity will either be infinity or negative infinity, depending on the leading coefficients. But since both leading coefficients here are positive (4 in the numerator and 3 in the denominator), the limit should be positive infinity. But wait, maybe I need to verify this more carefully.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should divide both numerator and denominator by the highest power of x in the denominator to simplify. The denominator's highest power is x\u00b2, so let's try dividing each term by x\u00b2. \n\nSo, the numerator becomes (4x\u00b3)/x\u00b2 + x\u00b2/x\u00b2 - 2/x\u00b2 = 4x + 1 - 2/x\u00b2. The denominator becomes (3x\u00b2)/x\u00b2 + 5x/x\u00b2 - 2/x\u00b2 = 3 + 5/x - 2/x\u00b2. Therefore, the expression simplifies to (4x + 1 - 2/x\u00b2)/(3 + 5/x - 2/x\u00b2). \n\nNow, as x approaches infinity, the terms with 1/x or 1/x\u00b2 will approach zero. So, the numerator behaves like 4x + 1 - 0 = 4x + 1, and the denominator behaves like 3 + 0 - 0 = 3. Therefore, the expression simplifies approximately to (4x + 1)/3. As x approaches infinity, 4x + 1 grows without bound, so the entire expression will approach infinity. Therefore, the limit is infinity. \n\nWait, but is that right? Let me think again. Maybe another way to look at it is to factor out the highest power in both numerator and denominator. For the numerator, which is degree 3, I can factor out x\u00b3: x\u00b3(4 + 1/x - 2/x\u00b3). The denominator is degree 2, so factor out x\u00b2: x\u00b2(3 + 5/x - 2/x\u00b2). Then the expression becomes [x\u00b3(4 + 1/x - 2/x\u00b3)] / [x\u00b2(3 + 5/x - 2/x\u00b2)] = x*(4 + 1/x - 2/x\u00b3)/(3 + 5/x - 2/x\u00b2). As x approaches infinity, the fractions with 1/x, 1/x\u00b2, etc., all go to zero, so this simplifies to x*(4 + 0 - 0)/(3 + 0 - 0) = (4/3)x. As x approaches infinity, (4/3)x clearly approaches infinity. So that confirms the earlier result. \n\nBut let me check with another method, maybe L\u2019Hospital\u2019s Rule. Wait, L\u2019Hospital\u2019s Rule applies when the limit is in an indeterminate form like 0/0 or \u221e/\u221e. In this case, as x approaches infinity, the numerator approaches infinity and the denominator approaches infinity as well because the leading term of the denominator is 3x\u00b2, which goes to infinity. Wait, but the numerator is degree 3 and denominator is degree 2, so as x approaches infinity, numerator grows faster, so the limit should be infinity. Wait, but L\u2019Hospital\u2019s Rule can be applied here since both numerator and denominator approach infinity, hence it's \u221e/\u221e. So applying L\u2019Hospital\u2019s Rule once: take derivatives of numerator and denominator. \n\nDerivative of numerator: d/dx [4x\u00b3 + x\u00b2 - 2] = 12x\u00b2 + 2x. Derivative of denominator: d/dx [3x\u00b2 + 5x - 2] = 6x + 5. So the new limit is (12x\u00b2 + 2x)/(6x + 5) as x approaches infinity. Let's check if this is still \u221e/\u221e. The numerator is 12x\u00b2 + 2x, which approaches infinity, and denominator is 6x + 5, which also approaches infinity. So apply L\u2019Hospital\u2019s Rule again. \n\nSecond derivative of numerator: d/dx [12x\u00b2 + 2x] = 24x + 2. Second derivative of denominator: d/dx [6x + 5] = 6. Now, the limit becomes (24x + 2)/6 as x approaches infinity. Simplify this: (24x)/6 + 2/6 = 4x + 1/3. As x approaches infinity, this expression clearly approaches infinity. Therefore, the original limit is infinity. \n\nSo all three methods\u2014dividing by x\u00b2, factoring out highest powers, and applying L\u2019Hospital\u2019s Rule twice\u2014lead to the conclusion that the limit is infinity. \n\nWait, but just to be thorough, let me consider another approach. Maybe comparing the growth rates of the numerator and denominator. Since the numerator is a cubic function and the denominator is a quadratic function, as x becomes very large, the cubic term will dominate over the quadratic term. Therefore, the numerator grows much faster than the denominator, so the ratio should grow without bound. Hence, the limit is infinity. \n\nIs there any chance I could have made a mistake here? Let me think. Suppose I miscalculated the derivatives when applying L\u2019Hospital\u2019s Rule. Let me check again. First derivative of numerator: 12x\u00b2 + 2x, correct. Denominator: 6x + 5, correct. Second derivative of numerator: 24x + 2, yes. Denominator derivative: 6, correct. Then (24x + 2)/6 simplifies to 4x + 1/3, which goes to infinity. \n\nAlternatively, if after the first application of L\u2019Hospital\u2019s Rule, we have (12x\u00b2 + 2x)/(6x + 5). Maybe simplifying this expression before taking the limit? Let's try dividing numerator and denominator by x: (12x + 2)/(6 + 5/x). As x approaches infinity, 5/x approaches 0, so the expression becomes (12x + 2)/6 = 2x + 1/3, which also approaches infinity. So even after one application, we can see it's going to infinity. \n\nTherefore, all approaches consistently show that the limit is infinity. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the concept of asymptotic equivalence. For large x, the numerator 4x\u00b3 + x\u00b2 - 2 is approximately 4x\u00b3, and the denominator 3x\u00b2 +5x -2 is approximately 3x\u00b2. Therefore, the ratio is approximately (4x\u00b3)/(3x\u00b2) = (4/3)x, which tends to infinity as x approaches infinity. \n\nTherefore, all methods converge to the same conclusion. The limit is infinity. \n\nWait, but just to make sure, maybe plugging in a very large x value to see. Let's take x = 1000. Then numerator is 4*(1000)^3 + (1000)^2 - 2 \u2248 4,000,000,000 + 1,000,000 \u2248 4,001,000,000. Denominator is 3*(1000)^2 +5*1000 -2 \u2248 3,000,000 + 5,000 \u2248 3,005,000. Then the ratio is approximately 4,001,000,000 / 3,005,000 \u2248 1331. So even at x=1000, the value is about 1331, which is a large number. As x increases, this ratio will keep growing, confirming the limit is indeed infinity. \n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and checks, I can confidently say the limit is infinity.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine \\(\\lim _{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{4 x^{3}+x^{2}-2}{3 x^{2}+5 x-2}\\), we analyze the behavior of the numerator and denominator as \\(x\\) approaches infinity. Here's the systematic breakdown:\n\n1. **Degrees of Polynomials**:  \n   - **Numerator**: Degree 3 (\\(4x^3\\) dominates).  \n   - **Denominator**: Degree 2 (\\(3x^2\\) dominates).  \n\n   Since the numerator's degree is higher, the limit tends to \\(\\pm\\infty\\). The leading coefficients are positive, so the limit is \\(+\\infty\\).\n\n2. **Simplifying by Dividing Terms**:  \n   Divide numerator and denominator by \\(x^2\\) (highest power in the denominator):  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{4x + \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{2}{x^2}}{3 + \\frac{5}{x} - \\frac{2}{x^2}}.\n   \\]  \n   As \\(x \\to \\infty\\), terms with \\(\\frac{1}{x}\\) vanish, reducing to \\(\\frac{4x}{3} \\to \\infty\\).\n\n3. **Factoring Dominant Terms**:  \n   Factor out \\(x^3\\) in the numerator and \\(x^2\\) in the denominator:  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^3(4 + \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{2}{x^3})}{x^2(3 + \\frac{5}{x} - \\frac{2}{x^2})} = x \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}.\n   \\]  \n   As \\(x \\to \\infty\\), this expression grows without bound.\n\n4. **L\u2019Hospital\u2019s Rule**:  \n   Apply twice for the \\(\\infty/\\infty\\) form:  \n   - First derivative: \\(\\frac{12x^2 + 2x}{6x + 5} \\to \\infty\\).  \n   - Second derivative: \\(\\frac{24x + 2}{6} = 4x + \\frac{1}{3} \\to \\infty\\).\n\n5. **Asymptotic Approximation**:  \n   For large \\(x\\), the ratio approximates to \\(\\frac{4x^3}{3x^2} = \\frac{4}{3}x \\to \\infty\\).\n\n**Conclusion**: All methods confirm the limit is \\(\\infty\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\infty}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. To find the limit \\(\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{4 x^{3} + x^{2} - 2}{3 x^{2} + 5 x - 2}\\), we start by dividing the numerator and the denominator by \\(x^3\\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4 x^{3} + x^{2} - 2}{3 x^{2} + 5 x - 2}\n   \\]\n\n2. Dividing both the numerator and the denominator by \\(x^3\\), we get:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4 x^{3} + x^{2} - 2}{3 x^{2} + 5 x - 2} = \\frac{\\frac{4 x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{x^{2}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{2}{x^{3}}}{\\frac{3 x^{2}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{5 x}{x^{3}} - \\frac{2}{x^{3}}}\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplifying the fractions inside the limit:\n\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{4 + \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{2}{x^3}}{\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{5}{x^2} - \\frac{2}{x^3}}\n   \\]\n\n4. As \\(x\\) approaches infinity, the terms \\(\\frac{1}{x}\\), \\(\\frac{2}{x^3}\\), \\(\\frac{3}{x}\\), \\(\\frac{5}{x^2}\\), and \\(\\frac{2}{x^3}\\) will approach \\(0\\). Hence, we get:\n\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{4 + \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{2}{x^3}}{\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{5}{x^2} - \\frac{2}{x^3}} = \\frac{4 + 0 - 0}{0 + 0 - 0}\n   \\]\n\n5. Since the numerator approaches a finite value \\(4\\) and the denominator approaches \\(0\\), the limit approaches infinity:\n\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{4 + \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{2}{x^3}}{\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{5}{x^2} - \\frac{2}{x^3}} = \\infty\n   \\]\n\n6. Conclusion:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\infty}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "17. Let \\(\\xi_{1}, \\ldots, \\xi_{n}\\) be independent and identically distributed random variables with a continuous distribution function \\(F=F(x)\\). Given that \\(\\mathrm{P}\\left(\\xi_{i}=\\xi_{j}\\right)=0\\) for \\(i \\neq j\\), show the following:\n\n(a) Prove that the distribution function \\(F_{r: n}=F_{r: n}(x)\\) of \\(\\xi_{r: n}\\) (the \\(r\\)-th order statistic) can be expressed via the incomplete beta function as follows:\n\n\\[\nF_{r: n}(x)=\\sum_{i=r}^{n} \\binom{n}{i} [F(x)]^{i}[1-F(x)]^{n-i} = \\frac{n!}{(r-1)!(n-r)!} \\int_{0}^{F(x)} z^{r-1}(1-z)^{n-r} dz\n\\]\n\nAssuming now that the distribution function \\(F\\) has a probability density function \\(f=f(x)\\):\n\n(b) Show that the probability density function of \\(\\xi_{r: n}\\) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{(r-1)!(n-r)!} F(x)^{r-1}[1-F(x)]^{n-r} f(x)\n\\]\n\n(c) The joint density \\(f_{r, s: n}(x, y)\\) of \\(\\xi_{r: n}\\) and \\(\\xi_{s: n}\\) (\\(r<s\\)) is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{(r-1)!(s-r-1)!(n-s)!} F(x)^{r-1}[F(y)-F(x)]^{s-r-1} F(y)^{n-s} f(x) f(y)\n\\]\n\nfor \\(x < y\\) and is zero otherwise.\n\n(d) The joint density \\(f_{(n)}\\left(x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{n}\\right)\\) of \\(\\xi_{1: n}, \\ldots, \\xi_{n: n}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nf_{(n)}\\left(x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{n}\\right)= \n\\begin{cases} \nn!f\\left(x_{1}\\right) \\ldots f\\left(x_{n}\\right), & \\text{if } x_{1}<\\ldots<x_{n} \\\\ \n0 & \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's tackle part (a) first. I need to show that the distribution function of the r-th order statistic, F_{r:n}(x), can be expressed either as a sum involving binomial coefficients or as an incomplete beta function integral. \n\nFirst, recall that the r-th order statistic, \u03be_{r:n}, is the r-th smallest value in the sample. So, the probability that \u03be_{r:n} \u2264 x is the probability that at least r of the \u03be_i's are \u2264 x. Since the \u03be_i's are independent and identically distributed with distribution function F(x), the number of \u03be_i's \u2264 x follows a binomial distribution with parameters n and p = F(x). \n\nTherefore, P(\u03be_{r:n} \u2264 x) = \u03a3_{i=r}^n [C(n, i) [F(x)]^i [1 - F(x)]^{n-i}]. That's the first part of the equality given. So that makes sense. \n\nNow, the second part is the expression involving the incomplete beta function. The incomplete beta function is defined as B(z; a, b) = \u222b_{0}^{z} t^{a-1}(1 - t)^{b-1} dt, and it's related to the beta function B(a, b) = \u0393(a)\u0393(b)/\u0393(a + b). \n\nI remember that the sum of binomial probabilities from i=r to n can be expressed using the beta function. There's a relationship between the binomial distribution and the beta distribution. Specifically, the sum \u03a3_{i=r}^n C(n, i) p^i (1 - p)^{n - i} is equal to the integral from 0 to p of [z^{r - 1}(1 - z)^{n - r}] / B(r, n - r + 1) dz, multiplied by B(r, n - r + 1). \n\nWait, let me verify that. The cumulative binomial probability P(X \u2265 r) where X ~ Bin(n, p) can be represented as the integral from 0 to p of the beta density. This is known from the relationship between the binomial and beta distributions. The formula is:\n\n\u03a3_{k=r}^n C(n, k) p^k (1 - p)^{n - k} = (1 / B(r, n - r + 1)) \u222b_{0}^p t^{r - 1}(1 - t)^{n - r} dt.\n\nMultiplying both sides by B(r, n - r + 1), which is \u0393(r)\u0393(n - r + 1)/\u0393(n + 1), but \u0393(n + 1) = n! So, B(r, n - r + 1) = ( (r - 1)! (n - r)! ) / n! ). Wait, actually \u0393(r) = (r - 1)! when r is an integer, right? So yes, B(r, n - r + 1) = ( (r - 1)! (n - r)! ) / n! )\n\nTherefore, the integral expression is (n! / [ (r - 1)! (n - r)! ]) \u222b_{0}^{F(x)} z^{r - 1} (1 - z)^{n - r} dz. \n\nSo, combining this with the binomial sum, we get that F_{r:n}(x) = \u03a3_{i=r}^n C(n, i)[F(x)]^i [1 - F(x)]^{n - i} = [n! / ( (r - 1)! (n - r)! ) ] \u222b_{0}^{F(x)} z^{r - 1}(1 - z)^{n - r} dz. \n\nThat should prove part (a). \n\nMoving to part (b), we need to find the probability density function (pdf) of \u03be_{r:n} given that F has a density f. Since the distribution function F_{r:n}(x) is given by the expression in part (a), the pdf f_{r:n}(x) is the derivative of F_{r:n}(x) with respect to x. \n\nLet's first compute the derivative of the sum expression. The derivative of \u03a3_{i=r}^n C(n, i)[F(x)]^i [1 - F(x)]^{n - i} with respect to x is \u03a3_{i=r}^n C(n, i)[i F(x)^{i - 1} f(x) [1 - F(x)]^{n - i} - (n - i) F(x)^i [1 - F(x)]^{n - i - 1} f(x) ].\n\nBut maybe it's easier to differentiate the integral form. Let's consider F_{r:n}(x