For vertex \\( A \\) with \\( \\angle BAC = 30^\\circ \\):\n\\[\nAH = 2R \\cos 30^\\circ = 2 \\times 2 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( AH \\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\sqrt{3}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's begin by analyzing the given triangle \\(\\triangle ABC\\) with the provided angle measurements and the length of side \\(BC\\).\n\n1. In \\(\\triangle ABC\\), it is given:\n    - \\(\\angle ABC = 80^\\circ\\)\n    - \\(\\angle ACB = 70^\\circ\\)\n    - \\(BC = 2\\)\n\n2. We need to recognize that point \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle ABC\\).\n\n#### Step 1: Identify angles at the orthocenter.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\angle HBC = \\angle ABC - \\angle ABH = 80^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 20^\\circ \\quad (\\text{since} \\; \\angle AHB = 180^\\circ - \\angle B \\; \\text{and} \\; \\angle B = 60^\\circ)\\\\\n&\\angle HCB = \\angle ACB - \\angle ACH = 70^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 10^\\circ \\quad (\\text{since} \\; \\angle CHB = 180^\\circ - \\angle C \\; \\text{and} \\; \\angle C = 60^\\circ)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 2: Apply sine law in \\(\\triangle BHC\\).\n\nUsing the Sine Law in \\(\\triangle BHC\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{BC}{\\sin \\angle BHC} = \\frac{BH}{\\sin \\angle HCB} = \\frac{HC}{\\sin \\angle HBC}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{\\sin 150^\\circ} = \\frac{BH}{\\sin 10^\\circ}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sin 150^\\circ = \\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{BH}{\\sin 10^\\circ}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n4 = \\frac{BH}{\\sin 10^\\circ}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nBH = 4 \\sin 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\n#### Step 3: Apply sine law in \\(\\triangle ABH\\).\n\nUsing the Sine Law in \\(\\triangle ABH\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{BH}{\\sin 80^\\circ} & = \\frac{AH}{\\sin 60^\\circ} \\\\\n\\frac{4 \\sin 10^\\circ}{\\sin 80^\\circ} & = \\frac{AH}{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin 80^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4 \\sin 10^\\circ}{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} = AH\n\\]\n\n\\[\nAH = 2 \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nConclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2 \\sqrt{3}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Show that every graph admits an acyclic orientation.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that every graph admits an acyclic orientation. Hmm, let me start by recalling what an acyclic orientation is. An acyclic orientation of a graph is an assignment of directions to each edge such that the resulting directed graph has no directed cycles. That means there's no sequence of vertices where you can follow the directions and end up back where you started. \n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. The task is to prove that no matter what graph I have\u2014whether it's connected or disconnected, simple or with multiple edges, etc.\u2014there exists a way to direct all the edges so that there are no cycles in the resulting digraph. That seems plausible, but how do I approach proving it?\n\nMaybe I can think about some examples. Let's take a simple graph, like a triangle (a 3-cycle). If I orient the edges in a cyclic way, like A\u2192B, B\u2192C, C\u2192A, then that's a cycle. But if I orient them differently, say A\u2192B, B\u2192C, A\u2192C, then there's no cycle. So for a triangle, there exists an acyclic orientation. Similarly, for a tree, which is already acyclic, any orientation will be acyclic because there are no cycles to begin with. But trees are special cases. What about graphs with cycles?\n\nWait, the key is that even if the original graph has cycles, we can choose directions for the edges such that those cycles aren't turned into directed cycles. So maybe there's a systematic way to do this. How?\n\nPerhaps using some kind of ordering of the vertices. If I can order the vertices in a linear sequence and then direct all edges from earlier vertices to later ones, that might prevent cycles. Because in such a case, all edges go one way, so you can't have a cycle where you go forward and then come back. But this would only work if the graph is a DAG (directed acyclic graph) to begin with. Wait, but any graph can be turned into a DAG by choosing such an orientation. \n\nWait, actually, if I take a graph and assign a linear order to its vertices, say v1, v2, ..., vn, and then direct every edge from the lower-numbered vertex to the higher-numbered vertex, then this orientation will definitely be acyclic. Because any directed path would have to go from lower to higher indices, so you can't have a cycle where you go from a higher index back to a lower one. \n\nSo that seems like a valid method. This is similar to taking a topological ordering of the graph. But wait, topological orderings exist only for DAGs. But here, we're constructing the orientation based on an ordering. So even if the original graph has cycles, by choosing an arbitrary linear order and orienting edges according to that order, we can avoid cycles. \n\nBut does this work? Let me test it on a cyclic graph. Take the triangle graph again. Suppose the vertices are labeled v1, v2, v3. If I direct all edges from lower to higher, then v1\u2192v2, v2\u2192v3, and v1\u2192v3. Wait, but there's no edge from v3 back to v1, so there's no cycle. So yes, that works. Similarly, for a 4-cycle, if I order the vertices v1, v2, v3, v4, and direct edges from lower to higher, then edges would be v1\u2192v2, v2\u2192v3, v3\u2192v4, and v1\u2192v4 (if it's a complete graph). Wait, actually in a 4-node cycle, the edges are (v1-v2), (v2-v3), (v3-v4), (v4-v1). If we direct them all from lower to higher, then we have v1\u2192v2, v2\u2192v3, v3\u2192v4, but the edge between v4 and v1 would be directed from v4 to v1 only if v4 is lower than v1, which it's not. Wait, but in a 4-node cycle, the edges are (v1-v2), (v2-v3), (v3-v4), (v4-v1). If we direct each edge from the lower-numbered vertex to the higher-numbered one, then the edge between v4 and v1 would be directed from v1 to v4 if v1 < v4. But in a cycle, the edge v4-v1 is between the highest and lowest indices. So if we direct it from v4 to v1, that would create a cycle. Wait, no\u2014if we follow the ordering strictly, the direction is from lower to higher. So for the edge between v4 and v1, since v1 is lower than v4, we direct it as v1\u2192v4. Then the cycle would have edges v1\u2192v2, v2\u2192v3, v3\u2192v4, and v1\u2192v4. Wait, but that's not a cycle. The cycle in the original graph is broken by the direction of the edge from v1 to v4. So in the directed version, there's no cycle. The original cycle is split into two paths from v1 to v4. So no directed cycle exists. \n\nTherefore, this method of ordering the vertices and directing edges from lower to higher indices seems to work. So in general, if we take any graph, assign a linear order to its vertices, and direct each edge from the earlier vertex to the later one, the resulting orientation is acyclic. \n\nIs there a name for this kind of orientation? It seems similar to a topological order, but here we are creating the orientation based on the order, rather than the other way around. In any case, the key idea is that such an orientation cannot have cycles because all edges go in one direction according to the order. \n\nBut let me make sure there's no loophole here. Suppose the graph has a cycle. When we direct the edges according to the vertex order, does it necessarily break the cycle? For example, consider a cycle with vertices labeled in the order they appear in the cycle: v1, v2, v3, ..., vn, v1. But if we assign a linear order that's different from the cyclic order. For example, suppose the cycle is v1-v2-v3-v4-v1, but we label them in the order v1, v2, v3, v4. Then directing edges from lower to higher would give v1\u2192v2, v2\u2192v3, v3\u2192v4, and the edge between v4 and v1 would be directed from v1\u2192v4 (since v1 < v4). Therefore, the original cycle is broken because the edge from v4 back to v1 is directed forward. So there's no directed cycle. \n\nAlternatively, if the vertices in the cycle are labeled in a different order. Suppose in a cycle of four nodes, the labels are v1, v3, v2, v4. Wait, no, the labeling is arbitrary. The key is that in any cycle, there must be at least one edge that goes from a higher-numbered vertex to a lower-numbered one if we follow the cycle. But if we direct all edges from lower to higher according to the linear order, that edge would instead be directed the other way, breaking the cycle. Therefore, no directed cycle can exist. \n\nAnother way to see it: suppose there is a directed cycle in the oriented graph. Then the cycle would have to consist of vertices where each edge goes from one to the next in the direction of the cycle. But since the vertices are linearly ordered, in such a cycle, there must be a highest-numbered vertex. The edge leading into this vertex would have to come from a lower-numbered vertex, and the edge leaving this vertex would have to go to a higher-numbered vertex. But since it's the highest in the cycle, there's no higher-numbered vertex to go to. Contradiction. Therefore, no directed cycle can exist. \n\nTherefore, this method works. So for any graph, pick a linear ordering of its vertices, orient each edge from the lower endpoint to the higher endpoint, and the resulting orientation is acyclic. \n\nBut wait, does this require the graph to be simple? What if there are multiple edges? Well, even with multiple edges, directing each edge from lower to higher would still prevent cycles, because multiple edges would all go the same direction. So even multigraphs can be oriented this way. \n\nWhat about graphs with loops? Wait, loops are edges from a vertex to itself. But in an acyclic orientation, can we have loops? No, because a loop is a cycle of length 1. Therefore, if the graph has loops, those cannot be oriented in any way other than themselves, so the orientation would not be acyclic. But the problem statement probably assumes simple graphs, or at least graphs without loops. \n\nWait, the original problem says \"every graph\". Hmm, but if the graph has loops, then it's impossible to have an acyclic orientation because a loop is a cycle. So maybe the problem is considering graphs without loops. Let me check the standard definitions. \n\nIn graph theory, a graph can sometimes allow loops, but often problems about orientations assume no loops. Since the question doesn't specify, perhaps we should assume that the graph is loopless. Otherwise, the statement is false. So assuming the graph has no loops, then the orientation method works. \n\nTherefore, the proof would go as follows: take any graph (without loops), assign a linear order to its vertices, direct each edge from the lower vertex to the higher one in the order. Since any directed cycle would require an edge from a higher vertex back to a lower one, which is not present, the orientation is acyclic. \n\nAlternatively, another approach might involve induction. Suppose we take a graph and remove a vertex, orient the remaining graph acyclically by induction, then add the vertex back and orient its edges appropriately. But I think the linear ordering method is simpler. \n\nWait, but maybe the problem expects a more graph-theoretical proof, perhaps using properties like choosing a maximal acyclic orientation or something. Let me think. \n\nAnother idea: any graph can be decomposed into a DAG by considering its strongly connected components. But I'm not sure if that helps here. \n\nAlternatively, we can use the fact that every graph has a vertex of degree \u2264 5 (but that's for planar graphs), which is irrelevant here. \n\nAlternatively, consider that if a graph has no edges, the orientation is trivial. If it has edges, pick an edge and orient it arbitrarily. Then continue orienting edges such that no cycle is formed. But this requires a process that ensures no cycles, which might be similar to building up a DAG edge by edge. \n\nBut maybe that's more complicated. The vertex ordering approach seems straightforward. \n\nWait, in graph theory, this is actually a known result. I think it's related to the concept of a \"linear extension\" of a partial order. If we take the vertices and order them linearly, then orienting edges according to the order gives a DAG. \n\nTherefore, the proof is as simple as assigning a linear order and directing edges accordingly. \n\nSo putting it all together, the answer is: Assign a linear order to the vertices of the graph. Direct each edge from the earlier vertex to the later vertex in the order. Since any directed cycle would require an edge from a later vertex back to an earlier one, which is not present, the orientation is acyclic. \n\nBut let me check once more. Suppose I have a graph with multiple cycles. For example, two separate triangles. If I order the vertices such that all vertices of one triangle come before the other, then directing edges from lower to higher would result in each triangle being acyclic, as before. If the vertices are interleaved, like v1 from the first triangle, then v4 from the second, etc., then edges within each triangle are directed according to the order, which would break the cycles. \n\nYes, in all cases, the linear order ensures that within any cycle, the highest-numbered vertex in the order cannot have an outgoing edge to a lower-numbered vertex in the cycle, hence breaking the cycle. \n\nTherefore, this method works universally for any graph (without loops), proving that every graph admits an acyclic orientation.", "deepseek_solution": "To demonstrate that every graph (without loops) admits an acyclic orientation, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Definitions:**\n   - An *acyclic orientation* of a graph assigns a direction to each edge such that the resulting directed graph contains no directed cycles.\n   - A *linear order* of vertices is a total ordering \\( v_1, v_2, \\dots, v_n \\).\n\n2. **Construction:**\n   - Assign a linear order to the vertices of the graph. This order is arbitrary and independent of the graph's structure.\n   - Direct each edge \\( (v_i, v_j) \\) from the vertex with the lower index to the higher index. Formally, if \\( i < j \\), orient the edge as \\( v_i \\to v_j \\).\n\n3. **Proof of Acyclicity:**\n   - Assume, for contradiction, that a directed cycle exists in the oriented graph. Let this cycle be \\( v_{k_1} \\to v_{k_2} \\to \\dots \\to v_{k_m} \\to v_{k_1} \\).\n   - By the orientation rule, each edge \\( v_{k_i} \\to v_{k_{i+1}}} \\) implies \\( k_i < k_{i+1} \\). Transitivity gives \\( k_1 < k_2 < \\dots < k_m \\).\n   - However, the cycle requires \\( v_{k_m} \\to v_{k_1} \\), which implies \\( k_m < k_1 \\). This contradicts \\( k_1 < k_m \\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - The contradiction arises from assuming a directed cycle exists. Hence, the orientation is acyclic.\n\n**Final Answer:**\nEvery graph admits an acyclic orientation. By assigning a linear order to the vertices and directing each edge from the earlier vertex to the later one in this order, no directed cycles can exist. This ensures the resulting orientation is acyclic.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's denote \\( G = (V, E) \\) as any graph with \\( n \\) vertices. We need to show that \\( G \\) admits an acyclic orientation.\n\n1. **Step 1: Number the vertices.**\n\n   Assign a unique label to each vertex of \\( G \\). Let \\( V = \\{v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n\\} \\) where \\( v_i \\) represents the vertex with label \\( i \\).\n\n2. **Step 2: Orient the edges.**\n   \n   For each edge \\( \\{v_i, v_j\\} \\in E \\), where \\( i < j \\), orient the edge from \\( v_i \\) to \\( v_j \\). This means we replace the undirected edge \\( \\{v_i, v_j\\} \\) with the directed edge \\( (v_i \\to v_j) \\).\n\n3. **Step 3: Verify the orientation is acyclic.**\n\n   To confirm the orientation is acyclic, suppose for the sake of contradiction that there exists a directed cycle in \\( G \\). A directed cycle would imply a sequence of edges \\( v_{i_1} \\to v_{i_2} \\to \\cdots \\to v_{i_k} \\to v_{i_1} \\) where \\( i_1 < i_2 < \\cdots < i_k \\) and \\( i_1 \\) must be both the smallest and the largest in the cycle, which is impossible.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   \n   Since our assumption of a cycle led to a contradiction, the orientation is indeed acyclic.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The graph } G \\text{ admits an acyclic orientation by orienting each edge from the vertex with the smaller label to the vertex with the larger label}.}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In an acute triangle \\( ABC \\), segments \\( BE \\) and \\( CF \\) are altitudes. Two circles passing through the points \\( A \\) and \\( F \\) are tangent to the line \\( BC \\) at the points \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) so that \\( B \\) lies between \\( C \\) and \\( Q \\). Prove that the lines \\( PE \\) and \\( QF \\) intersect on the circumcircle of triangle \\( AEF \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. I need to prove that the lines PE and QF intersect on the circumcircle of triangle AEF in an acute triangle ABC with BE and CF as altitudes. There are circles passing through A and F, tangent to BC at P and Q, with B between C and Q. Hmm, this seems a bit complex. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me draw a rough sketch of triangle ABC, making sure it's acute. BE and CF are altitudes, so E is on AC and F is on AB. Then, there are two circles passing through A and F, tangent to BC at P and Q. Since they are tangent to BC, their centers must lie along the perpendicular to BC at P and Q respectively. Also, since the circles pass through A and F, their centers should lie on the perpendicular bisectors of AF. Wait, but AF is a segment from A to F, which is on AB. Hmm.\n\nLet me recall that if a circle passes through two points, say A and F, and is tangent to a line BC at point P, then the center of the circle lies at the intersection of the perpendicular bisector of AF and the perpendicular to BC at P. Similarly for the other circle tangent at Q. So, for each circle, the center is determined by those two perpendiculars.\n\nGiven that, maybe I can find coordinates for all these points. Let me assign coordinate system to make things easier. Let's place point B at (0,0), C at (c,0), and since the triangle is acute, A is somewhere above the x-axis. Let me denote coordinates as follows: Let\u2019s set BC as the x-axis, with B at (0,0) and C at (c,0). Then, A is at (a,b), where a and b are positive numbers. Then, BE and CF are altitudes.\n\nFirst, find coordinates of E and F. Since BE is an altitude from B to AC, the equation of AC is needed. The coordinates of E can be found by projecting B onto AC. Similarly, CF is the altitude from C to AB, so F is the projection of C onto AB.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using barycentric or trilinear coordinates could help, but maybe Cartesian coordinates are more straightforward here.\n\nLet me proceed with coordinates. Let\u2019s set B at (0,0), C at (c,0), A at (a,b). Then, the equation of AC is from (a,b) to (c,0). The slope of AC is (0 - b)/(c - a) = -b/(c - a). Therefore, the equation of AC is y - b = (-b/(c - a))(x - a).\n\nThe altitude from B to AC is perpendicular to AC. The slope of BE is the negative reciprocal of the slope of AC, so m = (c - a)/b. Since BE passes through B(0,0), its equation is y = ((c - a)/b)x.\n\nTo find point E, which is the intersection of BE and AC. Let's solve the two equations:\n\n1. y = ((c - a)/b)x\n2. y - b = (-b/(c - a))(x - a)\n\nSubstitute equation 1 into equation 2:\n\n((c - a)/b)x - b = (-b/(c - a))(x - a)\n\nMultiply both sides by b(c - a) to eliminate denominators:\n\n(c - a)^2 x - b^2(c - a) = -b^2(x - a)\n\nExpand:\n\n(c - a)^2 x - b^2(c - a) = -b^2x + a b^2\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n(c - a)^2 x + b^2x - b^2(c - a) - a b^2 = 0\n\nFactor x:\n\nx[(c - a)^2 + b^2] - b^2[(c - a) + a] = 0\n\nSimplify (c - a + a) = c:\n\nx[(c - a)^2 + b^2] - b^2 c = 0\n\nTherefore:\n\nx = (b^2 c)/[(c - a)^2 + b^2]\n\nSimilarly, y = ((c - a)/b)x = ((c - a)/b)*(b^2 c)/[(c - a)^2 + b^2] = (c - a) b c / [(c - a)^2 + b^2]\n\nSo, coordinates of E are:\n\nE = ( (b\u00b2 c)/[ (c - a)\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ], (b c (c - a))/[ (c - a)\u00b2 + b\u00b2 ] )\n\nSimilarly, find coordinates of F. F is the foot of altitude from C to AB.\n\nEquation of AB: from A(a,b) to B(0,0). Slope is (0 - b)/(0 - a) = b/a. So equation is y = (b/a)x.\n\nThe altitude from C to AB is perpendicular to AB. Slope of CF is -a/b. Since it passes through C(c,0), equation is y = (-a/b)(x - c)\n\nFind intersection point F between AB and CF:\n\nSet (b/a)x = (-a/b)(x - c)\n\nMultiply both sides by ab to eliminate denominators:\n\nb\u00b2 x = -a\u00b2 (x - c)\n\nb\u00b2 x = -a\u00b2 x + a\u00b2 c\n\nBring terms together:\n\nx(b\u00b2 + a\u00b2) = a\u00b2 c\n\nx = (a\u00b2 c)/(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nThen y = (b/a)x = (b/a)*(a\u00b2 c)/(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) = (a b c)/(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nSo, coordinates of F are:\n\nF = ( (a\u00b2 c)/(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2), (a b c)/(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) )\n\nOkay, now we have coordinates for E and F. Next, we need to find points P and Q on BC (which is the x-axis from (0,0) to (c,0)) such that the circles passing through A and F are tangent to BC at P and Q, with B between C and Q. Wait, BC is from B(0,0) to C(c,0), so if Q is such that B lies between C and Q, that would place Q to the left of B? But BC is from B to C, so if Q is on BC extended beyond B. Similarly, P is another point on BC (possibly between B and C or extended). Wait, the problem says \"segments BE and CF are altitudes. Two circles passing through the points A and F are tangent to the line BC at the points P and Q so that B lies between C and Q.\" So Q is on BC extended beyond B, and P could be on BC or its extension. Hmm.\n\nWait, the p