2, so \u221a2/2 \u2248 0.7071\n\n0.8 - \u221a2/2 \u2248 0.8 - 0.7071 \u2248 0.0929\n\nSo slope = 0.4 / 0.0929 \u2248 4.306\n\nBut exact calculation:\n\nSlope m1 = (2/5 - 0)/(4/5 - \u221a2/2) = (2/5)/( (8/10 - 5\u221a2/10) ) = (2/5)/( (8 - 5\u221a2)/10 ) = (2/5)*(10/(8 - 5\u221a2)) = 4/(8 - 5\u221a2)\n\nRationalize denominator:\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (8 + 5\u221a2):\n\n4*(8 + 5\u221a2)/[(8 - 5\u221a2)(8 + 5\u221a2)] = 4*(8 + 5\u221a2)/(64 - 50) = 4*(8 + 5\u221a2)/14 = (32 + 20\u221a2)/14 = (16 + 10\u221a2)/7 \u2248 (16 + 14.142)/7 \u2248 30.142/7 \u2248 4.306, same as before.\n\nEquation of PE: y - 0 = m1(x - \u221a2/2)\n\nSimilarly, line QF connects Q(-\u221a2/2,0) to F(0.2,0.4). Compute its slope.\n\nSlope m2 = (0.4 - 0)/(0.2 - (-\u221a2/2)) = 0.4/(0.2 + \u221a2/2)\n\nConvert to exact fractions:\n\n0.4 = 2/5, \u221a2/2 \u2248 0.7071, so 0.2 + 0.7071 \u2248 0.9071\n\nBut exactly:\n\nSlope m2 = (2/5)/( (1/5 + \u221a2/2) ) = (2/5)/( (2/10 + 5\u221a2/10) ) = (2/5)/( (2 + 5\u221a2)/10 ) = (2/5)*(10/(2 + 5\u221a2)) = 4/(2 + 5\u221a2)\n\nRationalize denominator:\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (2 - 5\u221a2):\n\n4*(2 - 5\u221a2)/[(2 + 5\u221a2)(2 - 5\u221a2)] = 4*(2 - 5\u221a2)/(4 - 50) = 4*(2 - 5\u221a2)/(-46) = -4*(2 - 5\u221a2)/46 = -2*(2 - 5\u221a2)/23 = (-4 + 10\u221a2)/23 \u2248 (-4 + 14.142)/23 \u2248 10.142/23 \u2248 0.441\n\nSo equation of QF: y - 0 = m2(x + \u221a2/2)\n\nNow, find intersection point of PE and QF.\n\nLet me denote equations:\n\nPE: y = m1(x - \u221a2/2)\n\nQF: y = m2(x + \u221a2/2)\n\nSet equal:\n\nm1(x - \u221a2/2) = m2(x + \u221a2/2)\n\nSolve for x:\n\nx(m1 - m2) = m1*\u221a2/2 + m2*\u221a2/2\n\nx = [ \u221a2/2 (m1 + m2) ] / (m1 - m2 )\n\nFrom earlier, m1 = 4/(8 - 5\u221a2), m2 = 4/(2 + 5\u221a2)\n\nCompute m1 + m2:\n\n4/(8 - 5\u221a2) + 4/(2 + 5\u221a2)\n\nTo add these, find common denominator:\n\n(8 - 5\u221a2)(2 + 5\u221a2)\n\nMultiply out:\n\n16 + 40\u221a2 - 10\u221a2 - 25*(2) = 16 + 30\u221a2 - 50 = -34 + 30\u221a2\n\nSo, m1 + m2 = 4*(2 + 5\u221a2 + 8 - 5\u221a2)/[(8 - 5\u221a2)(2 + 5\u221a2)] = 4*(10)/(-34 + 30\u221a2) = 40/(-34 + 30\u221a2)\n\nWait, no, that approach is messy. Let me compute numerators:\n\n4/(8 - 5\u221a2) + 4/(2 + 5\u221a2) = 4*(2 + 5\u221a2 + 8 - 5\u221a2)/[(8 - 5\u221a2)(2 + 5\u221a2)] = 4*(10)/[(8)(2) + 8*5\u221a2 -5\u221a2*2 -5\u221a2*5\u221a2] = 40/[16 + 40\u221a2 -10\u221a2 -50] = 40/[ -34 + 30\u221a2 ]\n\nTherefore, m1 + m2 = 40/(-34 + 30\u221a2 )\n\nSimilarly, m1 - m2 = 4/(8 -5\u221a2) - 4/(2 +5\u221a2) = 4*(2 +5\u221a2 -8 +5\u221a2)/[(8 -5\u221a2)(2 +5\u221a2)] = 4*(-6 +10\u221a2)/(-34 +30\u221a2 )\n\nTherefore, x = [ \u221a2/2 * 40/(-34 +30\u221a2) ] / [4*(-6 +10\u221a2)/(-34 +30\u221a2) ]\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\nNumerator: \u221a2/2 *40 / (-34 +30\u221a2 )\n\nDenominator: 4*(-6 +10\u221a2 ) / (-34 +30\u221a2 )\n\nThus, x = [ \u221a2/2 *40 / (-34 +30\u221a2 ) ] * [ (-34 +30\u221a2 ) / (4*(-6 +10\u221a2 )) ]\n\nCancel (-34 +30\u221a2 ) terms:\n\nx = (\u221a2/2 *40 ) / (4*(-6 +10\u221a2 )) = (\u221a2/2 *10 ) / (-6 +10\u221a2 ) = (5\u221a2) / (-6 +10\u221a2 )\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (-6 -10\u221a2 ):\n\nx = 5\u221a2*(-6 -10\u221a2 ) / [ (-6 +10\u221a2 )(-6 -10\u221a2 ) ] = 5\u221a2*(-6 -10\u221a2 ) / [36 - (10\u221a2 )\u00b2 ] = 5\u221a2*(-6 -10\u221a2 ) / [36 - 200 ] = 5\u221a2*(-6 -10\u221a2 ) / (-164 )\n\nSimplify:\n\n= 5\u221a2*(6 +10\u221a2 ) / 164\n\nFactor numerator:\n\n= 5\u221a2*2*(3 +5\u221a2 ) / 164 = 10\u221a2*(3 +5\u221a2 ) / 164 = 5\u221a2*(3 +5\u221a2 ) / 82\n\nCompute this:\n\n= (15\u221a2 + 25*(2)) /82 = (15\u221a2 +50)/82 \u2248 (21.213 +50)/82 \u2248 71.213/82 \u2248 0.868\n\nSimilarly, y-coordinate using PE equation:\n\ny = m1(x - \u221a2/2 )\n\nFrom m1 =4/(8 -5\u221a2 )\n\nx \u22480.868, \u221a2/2 \u22480.707\n\nx - \u221a2/2 \u22480.868 -0.707\u22480.161\n\ny \u22484/(8 -5*1.4142)*0.161\u22484/(8 -7.071)*0.161\u22484/0.929*0.161\u22484.306*0.161\u22480.693\n\nBut exact value:\n\ny = [4/(8 -5\u221a2 )] * [ (5\u221a2*(3 +5\u221a2 ) / 82 ) - \u221a2/2 ]\n\nWait, this is getting too complicated. Maybe instead of computing numerically, find the equation of the circumcircle of AEF and check if the intersection point lies on it.\n\nCoordinates of A(0.5,1), E(0.8,0.4), F(0.2,0.4)\n\nCircumcircle of AEF: find equation passing through these three points.\n\nLet\u2019s use general equation of circle: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + Dx + Ey + F =0\n\nPlug in A(0.5,1):\n\n0.25 +1 +0.5D + E + F =0 \u21921.25 +0.5D +E +F =0 -- (1)\n\nE(0.8,0.4):\n\n0.64 +0.16 +0.8D +0.4E +F =0 \u21920.8 +0.8D +0.4E +F =0 -- (2)\n\nF(0.2,0.4):\n\n0.04 +0.16 +0.2D +0.4E +F =0 \u21920.2 +0.2D +0.4E +F =0 -- (3)\n\nSubtract equation (3) from (2):\n\n(0.8 +0.8D +0.4E +F ) - (0.2 +0.2D +0.4E +F ) =0 -0\n\n\u21920.6 +0.6D =0 \u21920.6D = -0.6 \u2192 D = -1\n\nPlug D = -1 into equations (2) and (3):\n\nEquation (2):\n\n0.8 +0.8*(-1) +0.4E +F =0 \u21920.8 -0.8 +0.4E +F =0 \u21920 +0.4E +F =0 \u21920.4E +F =0 -- (2\u2019)\n\nEquation (3):\n\n0.2 +0.2*(-1) +0.4E +F =0 \u21920.2 -0.2 +0.4E +F =0 \u21920 +0.4E +F =0 \u21920.4E +F =0 -- (3\u2019)\n\nSo equations (2\u2019) and (3\u2019) are the same, which is consistent. Now use equation (1):\n\n1.25 +0.5*(-1) +E +F =0 \u21921.25 -0.5 +E +F =0 \u21920.75 +E +F =0 \u2192E +F = -0.75 -- (1\u2019)\n\nFrom (2\u2019): 0.4E + F =0 \u2192F = -0.4E\n\nPlug into (1\u2019):\n\nE -0.4E = -0.75 \u21920.6E = -0.75 \u2192E = -0.75 /0.6 = -1.25\n\nThus, F = -0.4*(-1.25) =0.5\n\nTherefore, equation of circumcircle is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 -x -1.25y +0.5 =0\n\nConvert to standard form:\n\nx\u00b2 -x + y\u00b2 -1.25y = -0.5\n\nComplete the squares:\n\nx\u00b2 -x + (1/4) + y\u00b2 -1.25y + (1.25/2)\u00b2 = -0.5 +1/4 + (1.25/2)\u00b2\n\nCalculate:\n\nx\u00b2 -x +0.25 + y\u00b2 -1.25y + (0.625)\u00b2 = -0.5 +0.25 +0.390625\n\n\u2192(x -0.5)^2 + (y -0.625)^2 = -0.5 +0.25 +0.390625 = 0.140625\n\nThus, the circumcircle of AEF has center at (0.5,0.625) and radius \u221a0.140625 = 0.375\n\nNow, check if the intersection point of PE and QF lies on this circle.\n\nEarlier, we approximated the intersection point as (0.868,0.693). Let's check if this point is on the circumcircle.\n\nCompute distance from center (0.5,0.625):\n\n\u0394x =0.868 -0.5=0.368, \u0394y=0.693 -0.625=0.068\n\nDistance squared:0.368\u00b2 +0.068\u00b2\u22480.135 +0.0046\u22480.1396, which is close to 0.140625, considering rounding errors. So it's approximately on the circle.\n\nTherefore, in this specific case, the intersection point lies on the circumcircle of AEF, which supports the general statement.\n\nTherefore, the key insight is that the intersection of PE and QF lies on the circumcircle of AEF. To generalize this, perhaps using power of a point, cyclic quadrilaterals, or other circle properties.\n\nAlternatively, note that since PE and QF intersect on the circumcircle of AEF, their intersection point must satisfy the cyclic quadrilateral condition. Maybe showing that the power of the intersection point with respect to the circumcircle of AEF is zero.\n\nAlternatively, using radical axes. The radical axis of two circles is the set of points with equal power wrt both circles. But I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, using inversion with respect to the circumcircle of AEF. If the intersection point lies on the circumcircle, inverting might map lines to circles or vice versa, but this might complicate.\n\nAlternatively, consider that points A, E, F, and the intersection point are concyclic. So we need to show that \u2220AEX = \u2220AFX, where X is the intersection point.\n\nAlternatively, use coordinates for the general case. But that would involve heavy computation.\n\nWait, let's think of the problem in terms of cyclic quadrilaterals. If the intersection point X of PE and QF lies on the circumcircle of AEF, then quadrilateral AEXF is cyclic. So we need to show that \u2220AEX = \u2220AFX, or that \u2220XEA = \u2220XFA.\n\nAlternatively, use power of a point. For point X, if X lies