ows and white rows must form a majority for the columns. Since 99 is odd, one color must have a majority in the rows. Therefore, the next step of recoloring all columns will set them all to that majority color, which then propagates back to the rows. Therefore, this two-step process would result in a monochromatic grid.\n\nBut does this hold? Let's take an example. Suppose we have 50 black rows and 49 white rows after recoloring all rows. Then, each column has 50 black cells and 49 white cells. Therefore, each column's majority is black. So recoloring all columns to black would set every column to black. Then, each row, which is now made up of all black columns, is entirely black. Therefore, the entire grid is black. Similarly, if after recoloring all rows, there are 49 black and 50 white, then columns would be majority white, leading to an all-white grid. So this seems to work.\n\nBut the critical assumption here is that when we recolor all rows first, the resulting number of black and white rows has a clear majority (which it must, since 99 is odd). Then, the columns, being composed of these monochromatic rows, will have a majority color determined by the majority of the rows. Then, recoloring all columns to that majority color makes all columns (and hence all rows) that color. Therefore, this two-step process would always result in a monochromatic grid. Therefore, regardless of the initial configuration, by first recoloring all rows and then all columns, we can make the entire grid one color.\n\nBut wait, is this actually possible? Because when you recolor a row, you are changing the cells in that row, which are part of columns. Therefore, recoloring a row affects the column counts. Therefore, if you recolor rows one by one, each subsequent row's recoloring affects the columns, which might change the majority of other rows. Wait, but in the strategy above, we are recoloring all rows first, but in reality, when you recolor a row, you fix it to a color, and then mo