jority W)\n\nSo columns are all W. Then, recolor all columns to W, which does nothing. So grid is all W. So works.\n\nAnother example where after recoloring rows, columns have a different majority. Wait, but in the first step, after recoloring rows, all rows are W, so columns are all W. Therefore, it's already monochromatic.\n\nWait, maybe this always happens? If you recolor all rows to their majority color, and since each row is odd-sized, each row becomes monochromatic. Then, the columns are each composed of monochromatic rows. Therefore, the column's majority is simply the color that appears in most rows. Since there are an odd number of rows, one color must be in the majority. Therefore, all columns will be that color, leading to the entire grid being that color.\n\nWait, this seems to hold. Let me formalize it.\n\nSuppose we have a grid with m rows and n columns, both odd. Then, for each row, recolor it to its majority color. Let R be the number of rows that are recolored to black, and (m - R) recolored to white. Since m is odd, either R > (m - R) or vice versa. Suppose R > (m - R), so black is the majority row color. Then, each column, which now consists of R black cells and (m - R) white cells, since each cell in the column comes from a different row. Since R > (m - R), each column has a majority of black cells. Therefore, recoloring all columns to black results in all columns being black. Then, each row is composed of black columns, so all rows become black. Hence, the entire grid is black.\n\nSimilarly, if R < (m - R), then white is the majority, columns get recolored white, and the grid becomes all white.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the initial configuration, if you first recolor all rows to their majority color, then recolor all columns to their majority color (which will be the same as the majority row color), then the entire grid becomes that color.\n\nBut this seems to be a general result for any grid with odd dimensions. Therefore, in the case of a 99