 3), so recolor all columns to black. Then, all rows become black. But in reality, when I did the example, after recoloring the columns, the rows became B, W, B. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the example.\n\nWait, let's redo the example with the 3x3 checkerboard.\n\nInitial grid:\n\nRow 1: B, W, B\n\nRow 2: W, B, W\n\nRow 3: B, W, B\n\nRecoloring all rows:\n\nRow 1 has 2 B, 1 W \u2192 recolor to B \u2192 Row 1 becomes B, B, B\n\nRow 2 has 2 W, 1 B \u2192 recolor to W \u2192 Row 2 becomes W, W, W\n\nRow 3 has 2 B, 1 W \u2192 recolor to B \u2192 Row 3 becomes B, B, B\n\nNow, the grid is:\n\nB, B, B\n\nW, W, W\n\nB, B, B\n\nNow, columns:\n\nColumn 1: B, W, B \u2192 2 B, 1 W \u2192 majority B\n\nColumn 2: B, W, B \u2192 2 B, 1 W \u2192 majority B\n\nColumn 3: B, W, B \u2192 2 B, 1 W \u2192 majority B\n\nTherefore, recolor all columns to B. Then, the grid becomes:\n\nB, B, B\n\nB, B, B\n\nB, B, B\n\nTherefore, after two steps: rows then columns. So in this case, it took two steps. Wait, but in my previous mistaken example, I must have messed up the steps.\n\nSo perhaps in the 3x3 example, it does work in two steps. Then why did I think it took three steps before? Because I might have made a mistake in the column majority calculation. Let me verify.\n\nAfter recoloring rows:\n\nRow 1: B, B, B\n\nRow 2: W, W, W\n\nRow 3: B, B, B\n\nColumns:\n\nColumn 1: B, W, B \u2192 2 B, 1 W \u2192 majority B\n\nSimilarly for columns 2 and 3. Therefore, recolor all columns to B. Then, all cells become B. So yes, two steps.\n\nTherefore, my earlier mistake was due to an incorrect example. So in reality, the two-step process works even for the checkerboard pattern.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem, for a 99x99 grid, the strategy is:\n\n1. Recolor all rows to their majority color. This results in R black rows and (99 - R) white rows, where R is either \u2265 50 or \u2264 49.\n\n2. Recolor all columns to their majority color. Since each column now consists of R black cells and (99 - R) white cells, the majority of each column is black if R \u2265 50 and white if R \u2264 49. Therefore, after recoloring all columns, all columns become the same color (black or white), which in turn makes all rows that color as well. Hence, the entire grid becomes monochromatic.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the initial configuration, this two-step process will result in all cells being the same color. Therefore, the answer is yes, it is always possible.\n\nBut wait, in the two-step process, we have to assume that after recoloring all rows, the number of black rows R is either \u2265 50 or \u2264 49. Which is true because 99 is odd, so R cannot be exactly 49.5. Therefore, R is either \u2265 50 or \u2264 49. Therefore, the columns will all have a majority of either black or white, depending on R. Therefore, the next step of recoloring all columns will set them all to that color.\n\nTherefore, this seems to be a valid proof. Thus, the answer is yes.", "deepseek_solution": "**Step-by-Step Explanation and Answer:**\n\nTo determine if it's always possible to make all cells in a 99\u00d799 grid the same color using the allowed operations, follow this reasoning:\n\n1. **Operation Definition**: Recoloring a row/column sets all its cells to the majority color (pre-operation). Since 99 is odd, each row/column has a clear majority, avoiding ties.\n\n2. **Two-Step Strategy**:\n   - **Step 1: Recolor All Rows**: Convert each row to its majority color. Let \\( R \\) be the number of rows turned black (thus, \\( 99 - R \\) become white). Since 99 is odd, \\( R \\neq 49.5 \\), so either \\( R \\geq 50 \\) (black majority) or \\( R \\leq 49 \\) (white majority).\n   - **Step 2: Recolor All Columns**: Each column now consists of \\( R \\) black and \\( 99 - R \\) white cells. Depending on \\( R \\):\n     - If \\( R \\geq 50 \\), each column's majority is black. Recoloring all columns black results in an all-black grid.\n     - If \\( R \\leq 49 \\), each column's majority is white. Recoloring all columns white results in an all-white grid.\n\n3. **Propagation of Color**:\n   - After recoloring columns, every cell in each column becomes the majority color. Consequently, every row (composed entirely of column-colored cells) becomes uniformly that color. Thus, the entire grid becomes monochromatic.\n\n**Conclusion**: Regardless of the initial configuration, this two-step process ensures the grid can be made uniformly black or white. Therefore, it is **always possible** to achieve a monochromatic grid.\n\n**Answer**: $\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}$", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven a $99 \\times 99$ grid where each cell is either black or white, we are allowed to change the color of all cells in a row or column to either black or white, depending on the majority color of that row or column. The question is whether it is always possible to make all the cells in the grid the same color using these operations.\n\n1. **Initial Step:**\n   - Apply the described recoloring process to each row individually.\n   - This means, for each row, change all the cells in that row to either black or white, depending on which color is in the majority in that row.\n   \n   After this step, each row will be monochromatic (all cells in the row will be of the same color).\n\n2. **Observation:**\n   - Since each row is now monochromatic, the entire grid consists of rows that are either entirely black or entirely white.\n\n3. **Recoloring Columns:**\n   - Next, apply the same recoloring process to each column.\n   - As each row is already monochromatic from the previous step, applying the recoloring process to each column will result in making each column monochromatic.\n\n4. **Result:**\n   - Since the grid is already filled with either entirely black or entirely white rows, making each column monochromatic will ensure that the whole grid is the same color.\n   - Therefore, the entire grid will become monochromatic (all cells in the grid will be the same color).\n\n##