ircle passing through A and C.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps construct the midpoints of AB and AC. Let me denote M as the midpoint of AB and N as the midpoint of AC. Then the perpendicular bisectors of AB and AC are the lines perpendicular to AB and AC passing through M and N, respectively.\n\nTherefore, the perpendicular bisector of AB is the line through M perpendicular to AB. Similarly, perpendicular bisector of AC is through N perpendicular to AC. These two lines intersect AU at points V and W.\n\nSo, V is the intersection of the perpendicular bisector of AB with AU. Similarly for W.\n\nSo, perhaps we can find some relations between the distances AV, VU, AW, WU, and then relate them to TB and TC.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use coordinate geometry. Let me place the circumcircle on the unit circle in the coordinate plane. Let me assign coordinates to A, B, C, U.\n\nLet me suppose the circumradius is 1 for simplicity. Let me place point A at (1, 0). Then, since angle A is the smallest, points B and C are located such that the arc BC (not containing A) is the major arc. Therefore, points B and C are on the circumcircle, and the arc BC is greater than 180 degrees.\n\nLet me assign coordinates to B and C. Let me assume angle A is at (1, 0). Let me parameterize points B and C as (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8) and (cos \u03c6, sin \u03c6), where \u03b8 and \u03c6 are angles such that the arc BC not containing A is major. So, the arc length from B to C is greater than \u03c0, so the angle between them at the center is greater than \u03c0 radians.\n\nBut perhaps this is getting too complicated. Maybe there is a synthetic approach.\n\nSince V is on the perpendicular bisector of AB, VB = VA. Similarly, WA = WC. So, triangles VAB and WAC are isosceles.\n\nTherefore, angle VAB = angle VBA, and angle WAC = angle WCA.\n\nBut since point U is on the circumcircle, maybe there are some cyclic quadrilaterals here. Let's see.\n\nSince U is on the circumcircle of ABC, then angles subtended by the