xample says placing a piece on a shaded cell removes 4 cells marked with \u00d7 along with the shaded cell itself. So maybe the example is a smaller grid. Let's try to imagine. Suppose we have a grid where placing a piece on a certain cell removes that cell and all cells above and to the right. So, if the grid is, say, 3x3, placing a piece on (2,2) would remove cells (2,2), (2,3), (3,2), (3,3). So that's 4 cells. Then, if some cells were already removed earlier (dotted lines), the remaining cells after the move would be the ones not in the dotted lines or the \u00d7 marks. So in the example, the shaded cell plus 4 cells are removed. Hmm, maybe the example is in a different grid size, but the principle is the same.\n\nSo, back to the 5x7 grid. Each move removes a rectangle from (i, j) to (5,7). The players take turns doing this, and the person who removes the last cell loses because they have to remove it, right? Wait, the goal is to force the opponent to remove the last cell. So the loser is the one who has to remove the last cell. So if there's only one cell left, and it's your turn, you have to remove it and lose. Therefore, the game is similar to Nim, but with specific moves.\n\nThe problem is asking for the number of different possible situations that can appear during the entire course of the game. So, situations here refer to the possible configurations of the grid that can occur during the game. Each configuration is a subset of the original 5x7 grid, where some cells have been removed and others remain. However, not all subsets are possible, because the moves are restricted to removing rectangles from a chosen cell upwards and to the right. Therefore, the game's state is determined by which cells are remaining, but these remaining cells must form a \"downward-left\" closed shape. That is, if a cell is present, then all cells below it and to the left must also be present. Because once you remove a rectangle, you can't have a cell that's above or to the right 