rder ideal is a set where if you have an element, all elements below it are also included. So in our case, if (i, j) is in the order ideal, then all cells (k, l) with k \u2264 i and l \u2264 j must also be in the ideal. But in our game, the remaining cells are such that if a cell is present, all cells below and to the left must be present. Wait, that's exactly the definition of an order ideal in this poset. So yes, the remaining cells form an order ideal. Therefore, the number of possible game states is equal to the number of order ideals of the 5x7 poset under the component-wise order.\n\nBut how many order ideals are there for a 5x7 grid? This is equivalent to counting the number of monotonic boolean matrices or the number of plane partitions fitting in a 5x7 rectangle. Wait, plane partitions are 3D objects, but maybe it's related to the number of 2D Young tableaux. Wait, actually, the number of order ideals in a grid poset is given by the hook-length formula, but that's for linear extensions. Wait, no. Let me recall that for a poset that is a product of two chains (which is our case here: a 5x7 grid is the product of a chain of length 5 and a chain of length 7), the number of order ideals is given by the number of antichains, but that's not directly helpful.\n\nWait, actually, the number of order ideals in a product of two chains poset is a classic problem in combinatorics. The formula is given by the number of monotonic paths, but I might be mixing things up. Alternatively, the number is equal to the Delannoy number or the number of lozenge tilings. Wait, perhaps it's the number of tilings of a grid with dominoes? No, that's different. Wait, maybe it's the same as the number of Ferrers diagrams fitting into the grid. For a 5x7 grid, the number of Ferrers diagrams would be the number of integer partitions where the largest part is at most 7 and the number of parts is at most 5. But that's different from order ideals. Wait, no. If we consider the grid as a Young d