b - 3 = 10b^2 - b - 3\n\\]\n\n3. Subtract the right-hand side from the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n\\[\n(3b - 1)(4b + 1) - (2b + 1)(5b - 3) = (12b^2 - b - 1) - (10b^2 - b - 3)\n\\]\n\n4. Simplify the expression:\n\n\\[\n12b^2 - b - 1 - 10b^2 + b + 3 = 12b^2 - 10b^2 - 1 + 3 = 2b^2 + 2\n\\]\n\n5. Since \\( 2b^2 + 2 \\) is always positive for any real number \\( b \\):\n\n\\[\n2b^2 + 2 > 0\n\\]\n\n6. Thus, it follows that the original inequality holds for any value \\( b \\):\n\n\\[\n(3b - 1)(4b + 1) > (2b + 1)(5b - 3)\n\\]\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{true}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given a natural number \\( K > 2 \\) and a set of \\( N \\) cards, each with a positive number written on it. It turns out that it is possible to select some of these cards (possibly just one) with a sum of numbers equal to \\( K \\), some cards with a sum of numbers equal to \\( K^2 \\), ..., some cards with a sum of numbers equal to \\( K^K \\). Could it be possible that \\( N < K \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. The problem is asking whether it's possible to have a set of N cards with positive numbers such that for each exponent m from 1 to K (since K^1 up to K^K), there's a subset of cards that sums up to K^m. And the question is whether N can be less than K. Given that K is a natural number greater than 2.\n\nFirst, let's parse the problem again to make sure I understand. So, we need a set of numbers where for each m in 1 to K, there exists a subset whose sum is K^m. The question is if the number of cards N can be less than K. The answer is either yes or no, but we need to figure out which.\n\nLet me start by considering small values of K to get some intuition. Let's take K=3 first (since K>2). Then, the required sums are 3^1=3, 3^2=9, 3^3=27. So we need subsets that sum to 3, 9, and 27. The question is: can we have N<3, i.e., N=2 or N=1?\n\nIf N=1, then the only subset is the card itself. So